We investigate the existence of solutions to a system of Kirchhoff type equations of the form

$$\left\{
\begin{array}
[mbi]{rcl}
M\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^2}\left(|u|^p\right)^{\frac{2}{p}}dx\right)\Delta u+\lambda u=|u|^{m-1}u+\epsilon f(x) |u|^{m-2}u & \text{in} & \mathbb{R}^2,
\end{array}
\right.$$

where $1 < m < 2$ and $u \in H^1(\mathbb{R}^2)$, and find multiple solution alternatives under certain functional growth conditions for $f(x)$. Additionally, when $f(x)$ has sublinear growth, we establish the existence of a min-max solution. This work generalizes and extends the results of Li and Yang in [Li and Yang, J. Math. Anal. Appl., 2020]] for $M(k) = k$, and $p = 2$.

Researchers use maximum likelihood methods to estimate phylogenetic trees from genetic data, which are often represented as unordered trees with edge weights reflecting the evolutionary distances between species. In 2009, a significant breakthrough was achieved when a polynomial-time algorithm was developed to compare two unordered trees within a specific parameter class, known as the $M_{
eq}$-parameter, which includes the majority of current phylogenetic tree models. Subsequent research introduced the $M_{
eq}\$-distance parameter class, comprising tuples of distinct evolutionary rates, which significantly expanded the scope of applicable tree models. Although effective, current $M_{
eq}\$-distance maximum likelihood methods can be quite computationally expensive, which can hinder their practical application to real-world data sets. In response to this challenge, a new, highly efficient algorithm was recently proposed, capable of accurately comparing two unordered trees under any $M_{
eq}\$-distance parameter class within a specific subset of phylogenetic tree models. Building upon this breakthrough, the current study extends the algorithm's applicability to all current maximum likelihood methods used to analyze phylogenetic data, enabling fast $M_{
eq}\$-distance maximum likelihood tree estimation. A novel, novel algorithm is also introduced, which takes a user-specified tree and genetic data as input and outputs the maximum likelihood tree under any $M_{
